content.text:
verdict: clean
draft_version_seen: 3
checks:
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 (SAFe PM/PO)", question: "Do SAFe's PM and PO abstracts say what is quoted, and is the 'accept stories as done' line correctly marked secondhand?", source_reopened: "https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-management/ ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-owner/ ; https://www.globalknowledge.com/en/certifications/certification-training/safe/safe-product-owner---product-manager", result: pass, notes: "All abstract quotes verbatim; 'accept stories' absent from SAFe page and confirmed on Global Knowledge page verbatim ('is the only team member empowered to accept stories as done'); O'Reilly carrier not reopened; neither carrier URL is in the Sources list."}
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 (Pragmatic)", question: "Does Pragmatic say 'messenger of the market', 'development-facing role', 'run faster'/'right direction'?", source_reopened: "https://www.pragmaticinstitute.com/resources/articles/product/comprehensive-guide-product-owner-vs-product-manager/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; note 'messenger of the market' lives in the PO-vs-PM guide, not the role-of-PM article."}
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 (Cagan split)", question: "Do the PM-vs-PO articles contain 'very weak product', 'no clear owner', 'one person'?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/product-manager-vs-product-owner/ ; https://www.svpg.com/product-manager-vs-product-owner-revisited/", result: pass, notes: "All three verbatim."}
  - {claim: "summary 1 (Cagan 'backlog administrator')", question: "Does Cagan call the PM 'really a backlog administrator' in the Scrum-PO-class conception?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/behind-every-great-product/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim 'the product manager is really a backlog administrator'; the model described is escalate-everything-to-CEO, with Cagan's own next sentence tying the CSPO-class conception to that category — draft's gloss is a fair but compressed reading."}
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 (Scrum Guide)", question: "Does the Scrum Guide say 'one person, not a committee', delegation-but-accountable, 'respect their decisions'?", source_reopened: "https://scrumguides.org/scrum-guide.html", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F1 reconciliation", question: "Is the reconciliation marked as the report's judgment rather than sourced?", source_reopened: "n/a", result: pass, notes: "Labelled judgment, medium; consistent with Limitations."}
  - {claim: "F2 / summary 2 (Cagan four risks, start here)", question: "Are the feasibility/value-viability/outcomes/delivery sentences and 'problems to solve'/'output vs outcomes' verbatim?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/four-big-risks/ ; https://www.svpg.com/product-management-start-here/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; designer owns usability risk confirmed."}
  - {claim: "F2 / summary 2 (Perri)", question: "Do the secondary carriers say 'why, what to build, why it matters', 'own the roadmaps... priority', 'Agile does not have a brain', 'no measures of success'?", source_reopened: "https://www.produxlabs.com/product-thinking-blog/episode-252-project-product-management ; https://www.mindtheproduct.com/escaping-build-trap-melissa-perri/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; correctly marked secondhand; the 'Agile does not have a brain... told you what to build' ellipsis spans two sentences and is shown."}
  - {claim: "F2 (Pragmatic 'about the problem'; MTP competency)", question: "Are the 'tell the development organization about the problem' and 'no positional power' lines verbatim?", source_reopened: "https://www.pragmaticinstitute.com/resources/articles/product/role-of-product-management/ ; https://www.mindtheproduct.com/product-management-growth-team-effort/", result: pass, notes: "Both verbatim; MTP correctly marked abstract."}
  - {claim: "F3 / summary 3 (Scrum Guide PO accountabilities, sizing, DoD)", question: "Are the four backlog items, sizing sentence, and DoD sentence verbatim?", source_reopened: "https://scrumguides.org/scrum-guide.html", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim, including 'the Scrum Team must create a Definition of Done appropriate for the product'."}
  - {claim: "F3 / summary 3 (Cucumber three amigos, discovery, blog)", question: "Do the Cucumber pages say PO=scope, tester=scenarios, developer=steps, 'actively reviewed by the product owner', 'identify the problem', 'weak-ass move'?", source_reopened: "https://cucumber.io/docs/bdd/who-does-what/ ; https://cucumber.io/docs/bdd/discovery-workshop/ ; https://cucumber.io/blog/bdd/who-writes-the-cucumber-scenarios/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; 'identif[ies]' bracket is a legitimate inflection of 'identify the problem the team should be trying to solve'."}
  - {claim: "F3 (Humanizing Work)", question: "Are 'Invite a whole team...' and 'concrete change in system behavior' verbatim?", source_reopened: "https://www.humanizingwork.com/the-humanizing-work-guide-to-splitting-user-stories/", result: pass, notes: "Both verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F4 / summary 4 (Cohn)", question: "Does Cohn say 'what to build, not how' and 'sparingly, wisely and ideally, in consultation with the team'?", source_reopened: "https://www.mountaingoatsoftware.com/blog/can-a-product-owner-dictate-the-architecture", result: pass, notes: "Both verbatim (final paragraph)."}
  - {claim: "F4 / summary 4 (Wolpers)", question: "Is the 'Why/How/What' anti-pattern with 'Just stick with the Scrum Guide and its built-in checks & balances: The Developers answer the How' verbatim, and 'only person to decide what tasks become Product Backlog items'?", source_reopened: "https://age-of-product.com/product-owner-anti-patterns/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; draft's F4 elision after 'How' question' truncates mid-parenthesis but faithfully."}
  - {claim: "F4 / summary 4 (Xebia, SAFe architect/NFR, agileseekers)", question: "Are 'ultimately made by the product owner', 'valid business case', 'defining enablers... NFRs', 'revisited as part of the definition of done', 'tracked and prioritized in the backlog' verbatim?", source_reopened: "https://xebia.com/blog/architects-scrum-4-what-is-the-role-of-the-architect-in-scrum/ ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/system-architect/ ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/nonfunctional-requirements/ ; https://agileseekers.com/blog/collaborating-with-system-architects-to-refine-non-functional-enablers", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; SAFe pages confirmed login-walled beyond the abstract."}
  - {claim: "F5 / summary 5 (Torres trio)", question: "Are 'typically comprised', 'at a minimum', 'jointly responsible for a shared outcome', 'interview... choose... generate together', and the Cagan 'mercenary teams' attribution verbatim?", source_reopened: "https://www.producttalk.org/product-trio/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; the mercenary sentence begins 'This style of working also often results...' — draft's 'the hand-off model producing' is a paraphrase of the antecedent, acceptable since only the quoted fragment is in quotes."}
  - {claim: "F5 / summary 5 (Illustrated Agile, decode.agency, Aha!, LogRocket RACI)", question: "Are the feasibility/product-decisions, 'most important task', 'allocate capacity', and 'ONE single person' lines verbatim, and is the RACI described accurately?", source_reopened: "https://illustratedagile.com/2012/07/10/powerful-partnership-product-owner-designer-architects/ ; https://decode.agency/article/solution-architect-in-product-discovery/ ; https://www.aha.io/roadmapping/guide/product-development-methodologies/what-is-the-role-of-pm-in-safe ; https://blog.logrocket.com/product-management/sample-raci-chart-best-practices-template/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; RACI confirmed PM Accountable at all four stages, Architect/Technical Lead Responsible in Design only."}
  - {claim: "F6 / summary 6", question: "Are the feature-factory bullets, 'waiter... order taker', 'ticket monkey', 'waterfall 2.0', 'power struggles', and Spolsky quotes verbatim?", source_reopened: "https://blog.logrocket.com/product-management/build-trap-dangers-feature-factory-mindset/ ; https://lethain.com/notes-escaping-the-build-trap/ ; https://age-of-product.com/product-owner-anti-patterns/ ; https://www.meegle.com/en_us/topics/raci-matrix/raci-matrix-for-product-ownership ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architecture_astronaut", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; Larson's 'Leadership' is lowercase in source (capitalized at draft sentence start — trivial)."}
  - {claim: "F7 / summary 7", question: "Do Cagan and Torres actually assign usability/design its own owner, and is the gap marked as the report's observation?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/four-big-risks/ ; https://www.producttalk.org/product-trio/", result: pass, notes: "Designer owns usability risk (Cagan); designer is one of three minimum roles (Torres); gap correctly labelled as judgment."}
  - {claim: "summary 8", question: "Is item 8 declared a proposal with no confidence label and composed only from F1–F7?", source_reopened: "n/a", result: pass, notes: "Declared proposal, no label; content traces to F1–F7 plus existing role inputs."}
confidence_labels: "All defensible, with one conservative deviation: F4 is labelled medium although three sources were opened in full and one (Cohn) is primary, which the scheme would grade high; understating is not a false claim but the 'Medium' rationale text ('practitioner sources plus SAFe abstracts') should say why the primary is discounted or the label should be raised. No phrase mixes confidence with likelihood."
top_changes: ["Add the two secondhand carriers of the SAFe 'accept stories as done' line (Global Knowledge SAFe POPM page — verified verbatim this round; O'Reilly SAFe 4.0 Reference Guide ch. 18) to the Sources list with URLs and opened status, since F1 relies on them.", "Either raise F4 to high or state in its label why Cohn's primary does not lift it past medium.", "In summary item 1, tighten the 'backlog administrator' gloss: the quote describes the escalate-to-CEO model, to which Cagan then says the CSPO-class conception 'almost certainly' belongs."]
